WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.163
Say you write a small file, a manifest, that asks for two copies of nginx.

2
00:00:08.163 --> 00:00:11.024
nginx is a web server, handy for tests.

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.026
You type kubectl apply.

4
00:00:13.026 --> 00:00:15.726
And in under a second, it answers.

5
00:00:15.726 --> 00:00:16.361
Created.

6
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:18.114
So here is the question.

7
00:00:18.114 --> 00:00:20.813
At this very moment, is your app running?

8
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.325
It is tempting to picture kubectl as a remote control.

9
00:00:27.325 --> 00:00:33.355
You press the button, it reaches into a machine, starts your containers, and created means done.

10
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:34.588
Let us check.

11
00:00:34.588 --> 00:00:37.835
Two seconds later, ask kubectl get pods.

12
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.751
Zero of one ready.

13
00:00:39.751 --> 00:00:41.697
Still being created.

14
00:00:41.697 --> 00:00:42.184
Well.

15
00:00:42.184 --> 00:00:43.448
Created, yes.

16
00:00:43.448 --> 00:00:44.519
Cooked, no.

17
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.078
Here is a better picture.

18
00:00:47.078 --> 00:00:52.460
In our restaurant, a waiter takes your order, pins the ticket on the rail, and says got it.

19
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.826
Got it does not mean your soup is ready.

20
00:00:54.826 --> 00:00:56.955
It means your ticket is on the rail.

21
00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:58.871
Kubernetes has a rail too.

22
00:00:58.871 --> 00:01:03.483
It is the API server, the front door that every request goes through.

23
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:08.561
Its memory is etcd, a database that holds the whole cluster state.

24
00:01:08.561 --> 00:01:10.933
Only the API server talks to it.

25
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.111
Around the rail stand the staff.

26
00:01:13.111 --> 00:01:19.723
The floor manager, played here by two controllers: the Deployment controller and the ReplicaSet controller.

27
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:23.564
The head chef, the scheduler, who hands each dish to a station.

28
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:28.460
And at each station, which Kubernetes calls a node, a cook: the kubelet.

29
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.692
It never cooks with its own hands.

30
00:01:30.692 --> 00:01:35.551
It passes the job to containerd, which pulls images and starts containers.

31
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:38.651
Now, the one rule of this kitchen.

32
00:01:38.651 --> 00:01:40.082
Nobody calls anybody.

33
00:01:40.082 --> 00:01:42.604
Every part keeps one eye on the rail.

34
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.355
That is called a watch.

35
00:01:44.355 --> 00:01:50.237
It is one long request, and the API server streams every change down it, as it happens.

36
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.051
Step one.

37
00:01:51.051 --> 00:01:56.913
kubectl reads its config file to find the cluster's address and your credentials.

38
00:01:56.913 --> 00:02:00.169
Then it sends the manifest to the API server.

39
00:02:00.333 --> 00:02:00.994
Step two.

40
00:02:00.994 --> 00:02:05.617
The API server checks who you are, and whether you may do this.

41
00:02:05.617 --> 00:02:09.066
It validates the object, and stores it in etcd.

42
00:02:09.066 --> 00:02:12.221
Then it answers created, and kubectl exits.

43
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.608
Remember the guess?

44
00:02:13.608 --> 00:02:15.133
We are only at step two.

45
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:18.820
The ticket is on the rail, and no cook has touched it yet.

46
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:19.723
Step three.

47
00:02:19.723 --> 00:02:24.191
The Deployment controller sees the new Deployment through its watch.

48
00:02:24.191 --> 00:02:29.644
It creates a ReplicaSet, a helper object that keeps a set number of identical Pods.

49
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:30.479
Step four.

50
00:02:30.479 --> 00:02:35.571
The ReplicaSet controller sees the ReplicaSet, and creates two Pod objects.

51
00:02:35.571 --> 00:02:38.831
A Pod wraps a container and gives it an address.

52
00:02:38.831 --> 00:02:40.664
These two have no node yet.

53
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:41.471
Step five.

54
00:02:41.471 --> 00:02:44.356
The scheduler sees two Pods without a node.

55
00:02:44.356 --> 00:02:50.932
It filters out nodes that cannot take them, scores the rest, and writes a node name into each Pod.

56
00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:51.712
Step six.

57
00:02:51.712 --> 00:02:55.654
The kubelet on each chosen node sees a Pod given to it.

58
00:02:55.654 --> 00:03:00.025
It asks containerd to pull the image and start the container.

59
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:00.968
Step seven.

60
00:03:00.968 --> 00:03:02.712
The kubelet reports back.

61
00:03:02.712 --> 00:03:05.992
The Pod is running, and here is its IP address.

62
00:03:05.992 --> 00:03:09.272
The API server stores that in the Pod's status.

63
00:03:09.467 --> 00:03:12.505
Seven steps, and every one went through the rail.

64
00:03:12.505 --> 00:03:14.365
Nobody sent orders to anybody.

65
00:03:14.365 --> 00:03:16.783
Each part acted on what it found there.

66
00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:22.201
You might be thinking: wouldn't it be quicker if the scheduler just phoned the kubelet?

67
00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:23.779
Let us see why the rail wins.

68
00:03:23.779 --> 00:03:25.956
Think about the night something crashes.

69
00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:27.726
Say the scheduler stops.

70
00:03:27.726 --> 00:03:32.504
New Pods would wait in Pending, with no node, for as long as it is gone.

71
00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:36.357
When it comes back, it does not need to remember where it was.

72
00:03:36.357 --> 00:03:40.107
It reads the rail again and carries on, as if nothing happened.

73
00:03:40.107 --> 00:03:42.607
The rail remembers, so nobody else has to.

74
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.011
And if the rail itself goes down?

75
00:03:45.011 --> 00:03:49.299
kubectl fails, but the containers that already run keep running.

76
00:03:50.867 --> 00:03:53.768
You do not have to take my word for any of this.

77
00:03:53.768 --> 00:04:00.053
Each step leaves a short record called an event, and kubectl get events shows the whole chain, in order.

78
00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.961
Deployment controller, ReplicaSet controller, scheduler, then the kubelet.

79
00:04:04.961 --> 00:04:06.891
The same chain we just walked.

80
00:04:07.067 --> 00:04:11.150
And when something is stuck, the chain simply stops at one step.

81
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:16.509
A Pod that fits on no node stays Pending, with no node name, and its events say why.

82
00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:24.119
That is why, on a team, kubectl get events is often the first command anyone runs after a bad release.

83
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.759
So the moral of the story.

84
00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:29.532
kubectl apply hangs a ticket on the rail.

85
00:04:29.532 --> 00:04:31.832
Created means stored, not running.

86
00:04:31.832 --> 00:04:36.702
Then everyone watches the rail and does their part, a few seconds later.

87
00:04:36.702 --> 00:04:39.881
So after an apply, check with kubectl get pods.

88
00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:41.168
One quick check.

89
00:04:41.168 --> 00:04:44.886
The scheduler is down, and you apply a new Deployment.

90
00:04:44.886 --> 00:04:47.020
Does kubectl still say created?

91
00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:50.051
Yes.

92
00:04:50.051 --> 00:04:53.545
Step two only needs the API server and etcd.

93
00:04:53.545 --> 00:04:57.753
The Pods wait in Pending until the head chef is back.
